          "trace_id": "f6bd30f85fa940429ff285d46d7fa1bb",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-003",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "Migraines are a type of headache that can sometimes occur with an 'aura,' which can involve sensory changes. They can also occur without a headache at all.\n\nCommon triggers for migraines include irregular sleep, missed meals, dehydration, stress (and the period of relaxation after stress), hormonal changes around menstruation, alcohol, certain foods (in susceptible individuals), weather changes, bright or flickering lights, and the overuse of acute headache medication. Sleep patterns are particularly influential, as both too little sleep and sleeping in can trigger attacks, as noted in the `lifestyle-migraine-sleep` document. A headache diary can be useful for identifying individual triggers (from `condition-migraine`).\n\nGeneral management of migraines has three parts. Frequent use of acute medication can actually worsen headache patterns, potentially turning occasional headaches into chronic ones. This is known as medication-overuse headache. Guidelines suggest limiting acute medication use to fewer than ten days per month for certain medications (triptans, ergots, opioids, and combination analgesics) and fewer than fifteen days per month for simple analgesics. This is a common and reversible cause of chronic headaches and is checked before increasing preventive treatments (from `guideline-migraine-acute-and-preventive-treatment`).\n\nExperiencing migraines six times a month is a frequency that a person might reasonably discuss with a clinician to explore management strategies.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 8473.129943999993,
